<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="statistics-z-score" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>How to Calculate a <m>z</m>-score</title>

  <p>
    A <m>z</m>-score measures how many standard deviations a data point is from the mean, which allows direct comparisons across different datasets. This is useful for identifying unusually high or low values in settings such as test scores, physical measurements, and financial data.
  </p>

  <definition xml:id="statistics-zscore-definition">
    <title><m>z</m>-scores</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A <term><m>z</m>-score</term>, also called a <term>standard score</term>, measures how many standard deviations a data value is above or below the mean.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example xml:id="statistics-zscore-example-1">
    <title>Example 1</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider a normal distribution of heights with mean 175 cm and standard deviation 7 cm.
      </p>
      <table xml:id="statistics-zscore-table-heights">
        <title>Distribution of Heights</title>
        <tabular>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell>Height (cm)</cell>
            <cell>Empirical Rule Calculation</cell>
            <cell><m>z</m>-score</cell>
          </row>
          <row><cell>154</cell><cell><m>175+(-3\times 7)=154</m></cell><cell>-3</cell></row>
          <row><cell>161</cell><cell><m>175+(-2\times 7)=161</m></cell><cell>-2</cell></row>
          <row><cell>168</cell><cell><m>175+(-1\times 7)=168</m></cell><cell>-1</cell></row>
          <row><cell>175</cell><cell><m>175+(0\times 7)=175</m></cell><cell>0</cell></row>
          <row><cell>182</cell><cell><m>175+(1\times 7)=182</m></cell><cell>1</cell></row>
          <row><cell>189</cell><cell><m>175+(2\times 7)=189</m></cell><cell>2</cell></row>
          <row bottom="major"><cell>196</cell><cell><m>175+(3\times 7)=196</m></cell><cell>3</cell></row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
      <p>
        Notice the multiplier on <m>\sigma</m> equals the <m>z</m>-score. So, a <m>z</m>-score directly counts how many standard deviations a value is above or below the mean.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="statistics-zscore-example-1-graph">
        <caption>Normal distribution with heights and z-scores labeled on the axis.</caption>
        <image source="statistics/recreated_zscores.png" decorative="yes"/>
      </figure>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <definition xml:id="statistics-zscore-formula">
    <title>How to Calculate a <m>z</m>-score</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The general structure is:
      </p>
      <p><m>z=\dfrac{\text{data}-\text{mean}}{\text{standard deviation}}</m></p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <definition xml:id="statistics-zscore-context-formulas">
    <title><m>z</m>-Score for Different Contexts</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <em>Individuals in a Population:</em>
            <m>z=\dfrac{x-\mu}{\sigma}</m>
          </li>
          <li>
            <em>Individuals in a Sample:</em>
            <m>z=\dfrac{x-\overline{x}}{s}</m>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="statistics-zscore-context-figure">
        <caption>Two normal distributions: one on the original scale and one on the z-score scale.</caption>
        <image source="statistics/zscores2.png" decorative="yes"/>
      </figure>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <definition xml:id="statistics-zscore-features">
    <title>Important Features of <m>z</m>-Scores</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><m>z</m>-scores measure distance from the mean and have no units.</li>
          <li>A positive <m>z</m>-score means the value is above the mean.</li>
          <li>A negative <m>z</m>-score means the value is below the mean.</li>
          <li>A value is significantly low if <m>z&lt;-2</m>.</li>
          <li>A value is significantly high if <m>z&gt;2</m>.</li>
          <li><m>z</m>-scores allow relative comparisons across different datasets.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example xml:id="statistics-zscore-example-2">
    <title>Example 2</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Men’s and women’s heights are both bell-shaped. Mean and standard deviation are:
      </p>
      <table xml:id="statistics-zscore-heights-table-2">
        <title>Average Heights and Standard Deviations</title>
        <tabular>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell>Group</cell>
            <cell>Mean Height (in)</cell>
            <cell>Standard Deviation (in)</cell>
          </row>
          <row><cell>Men</cell><cell>69.9</cell><cell>3.0</cell></row>
          <row bottom="major"><cell>Women</cell><cell>64.3</cell><cell>2.6</cell></row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
      <p>
        Who is relatively taller: a man or a woman who is 5'8" (68 inches)?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p><em>Step 1: Compute z-scores</em></p>
      <p><m>z_{\text{men}}=\dfrac{68-69.9}{3.0}=\dfrac{-1.9}{3.0}\approx -0.63</m></p>
      <p><m>z_{\text{women}}=\dfrac{68-64.3}{2.6}=\dfrac{3.7}{2.6}\approx 1.42</m></p>
      <p><em>Step 2: Interpret</em></p>
      <p>
        For men, 68 inches is below average; for women, 68 inches is above average. Since the woman’s z-score is larger, a 5'8" woman is relatively taller than a 5'8" man.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="statistics-zscore-example-3">
    <title>Example 3</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The ACT has mean 20.8 and standard deviation 5.8. The SAT has mean 1500 and standard deviation 300. Who performed worse relative to peers: SAT score 1075 or ACT score 15?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p><em>Step 1: Compute z-scores</em></p>
      <p><m>z_{\text{SAT}}=\dfrac{1075-1500}{300}=\dfrac{-425}{300}\approx -1.42</m></p>
      <p><m>z_{\text{ACT}}=\dfrac{15-20.8}{5.8}=\dfrac{-5.8}{5.8}=-1.00</m></p>
      <p><em>Step 2: Interpret</em></p>
      <p>
        The SAT z-score is lower, so the SAT student performed worse relative to peers.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <definition xml:id="statistics-zscore-extreme-values">
    <title>Extreme Values</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> be values from one or more normal distributions. Value <m>x</m> is more extreme than <m>y</m> if its distance from its mean (in standard deviation units) is greater.
      </p>
      <p>
        To compare extremeness, use absolute z-scores:
      </p>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><em>Step 1:</em> Compute both z-scores.</li>
          <li><em>Step 2:</em> Take absolute values.</li>
          <li><em>Step 3:</em> Larger absolute z-score means more extreme value.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example xml:id="statistics-zscore-example-4">
    <title>Example 4</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Men’s weights in their 30s: mean 208 lbs, standard deviation 25 lbs. Women’s weights in their 30s: mean 175 lbs, standard deviation 20 lbs. Which is more extreme: a man who weighs 155 lbs or a woman who weighs 224 lbs?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>Compute absolute z-scores.</p>
      <p><em>Man (155 lbs):</em> <m>\left|\dfrac{155-208}{25}\right|=\left|\dfrac{-53}{25}\right|=2.12</m></p>
      <p><em>Woman (224 lbs):</em> <m>\left|\dfrac{224-175}{20}\right|=\left|\dfrac{49}{20}\right|=2.45</m></p>
      <p>
        Since <m>2.45&gt;2.12</m>, the woman’s weight is more extreme.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="statistics-zscore-example-4-man">
        <caption>Man: distance from z = -2.12 to z = 0.</caption>
        <image source="statistics/zscores3.png" decorative="yes"/>
      </figure>
      <figure xml:id="statistics-zscore-example-4-woman">
        <caption>Woman: distance from z = 0 to z = 2.45.</caption>
        <image source="statistics/zscores4.png" decorative="yes"/>
      </figure>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <p>
    <m>z</m>-scores provide a standardized method to compare values within and across distributions by measuring how many standard deviations a value is from the mean. They are essential for identifying extreme values, assessing relative standing, and preparing for later topics involving probabilities, confidence intervals, and hypothesis tests.
  </p>
</section>